## Core Modules

GUTS IAM is structured into independent yet interconnected modules. Each module is responsible for a specific domain of identity, access control, or security management, ensuring separation of concerns and scalability.

---

### Authentication Module

Responsible for verifying user identity and securing access to the system.

- Handles user login and credential validation
- Generates and validates JWT tokens
- Integrates with Redis for session and token management
- Supports secure logout and token invalidation

---

### User Management Module

Manages user lifecycle and profile operations.

- User registration and onboarding
- Profile updates and account management
- Account activation and deactivation
- Maintains user status and metadata

---

### Role Management Module

Defines and manages access roles within the system.

- Create and update roles
- Assign roles to users
- Organize access levels based on responsibilities
- Acts as a bridge between users and permissions

---

### Permission Management Module

Controls fine-grained authorization rules.

- Define system permissions
- Assign permissions to roles
- Enforce access control during request processing
- Ensures users only access allowed resources

---

### Audit Logging Module

Tracks system activity for monitoring and accountability.

- Records user and admin actions
- Stores security-relevant events
- Maintains historical logs for analysis
- Supports log export for reporting and compliance

---

### Security Enhancement Module

Provides additional security layers beyond basic IAM functionality.

- Email verification during user registration
- Password reset and recovery via email
- Location-based login tracking (security signal layer)
- Supports future fraud detection integration (ML-ready design)

---

### Session Management Module

Handles user session lifecycle and security control.

- Session tracking using Redis
- Token expiration and refresh handling
- Secure session invalidation
- Prevents unauthorized session reuse
